                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Most point-and-click adventures tether you firmly to the ground, relying entirely on static screens and a trusty cursor. Journey of a Roach subverts this immediately with a genuinely brilliant traversal mechanic. As the titular insect, you aren't just scuttling across the floor; you can seamlessly transition to walking up walls and across ceilings. It is a charmingly funny adventure where this added verticality actively enriches the exploration. By opening up the environments in full 3D space, it makes the simple act of navigating the world significantly more engaging than your standard genre fare.\r\n\r\nWhen it comes to puzzle design, the point-and-click genre is infamous for its frustrating leaps of \"moon logic,\" but this game is a refreshing exception. The puzzles here are undeniably tough, yet they remain fundamentally logical. I found a lot of genuine satisfaction in figuring them out, primarily because the solutions are incredibly coherent with the bizarre, post-apocalyptic, insect-sized world they inhabit. The game’s comedic tone isn't just window dressing for the narrative; it bleeds directly into the puzzle logic itself. You are never forced into absurd combinations for the sake of artificial difficulty. Instead, the solutions make a twisted sort of sense for a scrappy little roach, which is always a massive positive.\r\n\r\nPerhaps its greatest strength, however, is its brevity. Clocking in at a maximum of three hours, Journey of a Roach is exactly as long as it needs to be. In an era where titles constantly demand dozens of hours to pad out their runtime, a tightly paced, single-sitting experience is highly commendable. It tells its joke, delivers its clever puzzles, and rolls the credits before the wall-crawling mechanics have any chance to go stale.\r\n\r\nIt is a fantastic little palate cleanser, and it ran flawlessly on the Steam Deck.",
